6. Data attribution for the speculative-decoding drafter (the PLaMo-port drafter package named in release.json)
================================================================================

The drafter is a derived model of PLaMo 3 Translate: it was trained on PLaMo 3 Translate's own greedy translations
of source sentences taken from the permissively licensed corpora below, and is therefore distributed under the PLaMo
Community License (Article 5). Only the source side of these public corpora was used as input; no source text is
distributed, only trained weights, token-id tables and numeric test vectors. Licences and attributions:

- Tatoeba (https://tatoeba.org): "Sentences from Tatoeba, contributed by the Tatoeba community under CC BY 2.0 FR"
  (Creative Commons Attribution 2.0 France, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/fr/; sentences that Tatoeba
  lists as CC0 are CC0 1.0). The contributor of every sentence that was used is recorded per record in the attribution
  file shipped with the drafter package: plamo3-drafter-port-v7-attribution/PLaMo-ATTRIBUTION-D20.jsonl.gz (one JSON
  line per training request: licence, attribution string "tatoeba.org #<sentence id> (<contributor>)", source URL; no
  text). The file is covered by SHA256SUMS and by the installer's verification.
- OpenAssistant Conversations datasets OASST1 and OASST2, LAION / the Open-Assistant contributors, licensed under the
  Apache License, Version 2.0 (https://huggingface.co/datasets/OpenAssistant/oasst1, .../oasst2). Message id and role
  per record are in the attribution file; the Apache-2.0 notice applies to the portions used.
- e-Gov Law Search (e-Gov 法令検索, https://laws.e-gov.go.jp/), Digital Agency of Japan: statutes and orders are not
  protected by copyright (Japanese Copyright Act Article 13(i)); published under the Government Standard Terms of Use
  2.0 (compatible with CC BY 4.0). Source: e-Gov Law Search, Digital Agency.
- Aozora Bunko (青空文庫, https://www.aozora.gr.jp/): works in the public domain (author died in 1967 or earlier, first
  published in 1930 or earlier); the texts were prepared by the Aozora Bunko volunteers.
